# WHITELIST REPO — everything is untracked unless re-included below
#
# THIS FILE SERVES TWO TREES, and reading it as one list is how it gets broken (#136). It governs
# THIS repository, where every file now lives under estate/, under dev/, or in the root-pinned set;
# and it SHIPS as an estate's own whitelist, where the laid-down tree has no estate/ prefix at all.
# So the re-includes come in two blocks, and each block is dead in the other tree BY DESIGN:
#   * the REPOSITORY block re-includes the two trees and the root-pinned files. In an installed
#     estate estate/ and dev/ do not exist, so those two lines match nothing there.
#   * the ESTATE block re-includes the estate's own top-level record set. In THIS repository those
#     paths sit under estate/, so these lines match nothing here — they are not stale, they are the
#     shipped contract, and deleting them would leave every installed estate ignoring its records.
# A re-include whose subject has left BOTH roots is deleted rather than left as a false claim; the
# development surfaces that moved into dev/ (the method doc, the decision records, the loop
# templates, the history folder, the roster review) were exactly that, and their lines are gone.
# A re-include whose subject exists in only ONE tree lives in THAT tree's block, and a DEVELOPMENT
# surface gets no line at all (#153) — an estate's whitelist admits an estate's records, nothing
# else. The five root-pinned lines below are the exception that proves it: they are the exact five
# files install.sh ships from outside the estate tree (README.md, AGENTS.md, LICENSE, .gitignore,
# .gitattributes — see derive_product_paths), so each one is a record in BOTH trees.

# ADD THE LINE BEFORE YOU ADD THE FILE — a new top-level file or folder that no line below names
# is skipped by `git add -A` in SILENCE, so the commit and the push both succeed without it, and
# `git add <path>` is the only command that will tell you.
/*
!/.gitignore
!/.gitattributes
!/LICENSE
!/README.md
!/AGENTS.md

# `/CLAUDE.md` IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT (#153), and its absence is easier to misread as an omission
# than as a decision. It is this repository's own development rule file; the installer never lays
# it down, so a re-include for it was a development surface sitting in every installed estate's
# whitelist, ready to sweep a stray copy from a botched install into a user's record. THIS
# REPOSITORY IS UNHARMED: an ignore rule does not untrack, and CLAUDE.md was tracked long before
# the line went, so it stays in the record and its edits still commit normally. Only re-ADDING it
# after a delete would need `git add -f`. A NEW development file belongs under dev/, which is
# re-included whole below and costs nothing.

# THE PLATFORM DIRECTORY IS NARROWED, NOT RE-INCLUDED WHOLE (#153). `.github/` exists in both trees
# and holds entirely different things in each: this repository's CI and issue forms, and an
# installed estate's GENERATED hook configuration. A blanket `!/.github/` therefore admitted the
# first set into every estate's whitelist. REMOVING the line was the tempting wrong answer — the
# hook config lives beneath it, and an estate that stops tracking its own hook settings drifts with
# nothing to say so. So the directory is opened and then closed again on the next line, and each
# tree re-admits its OWN half below: this repository's in the REPOSITORY block, the estate's in the
# ESTATE block. Both halves are named, because a path is all git has to tell the two trees apart.
!/.github/
/.github/*

# --- REPOSITORY block — the two trees this repository is split into (#136) ----------------------
!/estate/
!/dev/
# This repository's half of the platform directory: the acceptance-demo workflow, the issue forms,
# the PR template. An installed estate has none of these, so these lines match nothing there.
!/.github/workflows/
!/.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/
!/.github/pull_request_template.md

# --- ESTATE block — the record set an installed estate re-includes at ITS root -------------------
!/SPEC.md
!/AI-SETUP-PROMPT.md
!/CONSTITUTION.md
!/folder-map.md
!/INSTALL-INSTRUCTIONS.md
# The worked example — the day-in-the-life document a newcomer reads (#153). It became a PRODUCT
# file by MOVING under estate/, because classification is derived from the tree (derive_product_paths
# in install.sh) and there is no manifest row to add any more. This line is the other half of that
# move and the half with no symptom: without it the document lands at every estate's root, on disk
# and OUTSIDE the record, while the estate goes on reporting healthy.
!/WORKED-EXAMPLE.md
!/install.sh
!/VERSION
!/_agents/
!/_harness/
!/Tickets/
!/General AI-Knowledge/
!/General Human Knowledge/
# ^ the estate's root DOCUMENTS and folders end here: a newly shipped estate-root file adds its
# line directly above this comment, and needs nothing else. What follows is the same block's
# platform half, which is a different kind of subject and is kept apart on purpose.

# The estate's half of the platform directory: the hook configuration install.sh WRITES at install
# time by copying _harness/hooks/hooks.example.json to .github/hooks/harness.json. It is the one
# thing under .github/ that belongs to an estate, and it is in neither the shipped tree nor any
# manifest — it does not exist until an install creates it, which is why the installer, not a file
# list, is the authority on this line. If it falls out of the record the estate's auto-commit
# settings drift silently. The DIRECTORY is named rather than the single file, so a second
# generated hook config is inside the record the day it is written.
!/.github/hooks/
# The upgrade's QUARANTINE folder, where install.sh --upgrade moves a superseded or replaced file
# instead of deleting it (#134). Said out loud rather than left to the deny-all above, because
# "untracked" is a DECIDED property here, not an accident: it is the reason every retirement is
# reported to the user at the moment it happens, with the command that puts it back. A future
# re-include that reached it would silently sweep a user's retired copies into the record.
/_retired/

# working bulk inside tickets is never a record. TWO SPELLINGS, because a pattern carrying a
# separator is anchored to this file's own directory: the bare form is the estate's, the
# estate/-prefixed form is this repository's, and neither reaches the other's tree.
Tickets/*/Logs/*
Tickets/*/Dump/*
!Tickets/*/Logs/.gitkeep
!Tickets/*/Dump/.gitkeep
estate/Tickets/*/Logs/*
estate/Tickets/*/Dump/*
!estate/Tickets/*/Logs/.gitkeep
!estate/Tickets/*/Dump/.gitkeep

# OS noise + objective editor/tool junk — never records, safe to ignore everywhere (no data
# extensions: the user's own files stay tracked/sovereign). .DS_Store already covered above.
.DS_Store
**/.DS_Store
*.tmp
*~
*.swp
*.swo
Thumbs.db
